---
title: "Peripheral Transcriptomic Reframing of Fibromyalgia: ECM / Neurite-Outgrowth Dysregulation, Compositional Neuropeptide Shift, and pH/Nociception Dysregulation"
author: 
  - "Investigación Computacional DAVI"
  - "Eidos Lab Research"
date: "2026-08-14"
abstract: "Background: Fibromyalgia (FM) is a chronic pain syndrome of unknown molecular etiology. Methods: Targeted reanalysis of two public transcriptomic cohorts (GSE221921 PBMCs, GSE67311 whole blood) informed by population-scale UK Biobank plasma proteomics (2,923 proteins, 51,644 participants). Nine falsification methods applied: permutation, winsorization, leave-one-out, deconvolution, Bonferroni, sex stratification, cell-composition adjustment, negative controls, and E-value. Results: (1) An extracellular-matrix / neurite-outgrowth module (COL9A1 FC=2.32 d=+0.88; PTN FC=2.91 d=+0.61) survives all nine falsification tests simultaneously; COL9A1 E-value=8.98, robust to moderate confounding. PTN downgraded to secondary candidate (falsified by NNLS deconvolution, p=0.076). (2) A complete opioid/tachykinin neuropeptide block elevated compositionally (TACR1 d=+0.60, OPRM1 d=+0.53) — reflects circulating leukocyte shift, not per-cell activation. (3) CA14 (top UKB causal protein for chronic widespread pain) is sex-confounded in PBMCs and downregulated in plasma. (4) Five UKB-causal immune genes downregulated (immunomodulation, not inflammation). Conclusions: The FM peripheral signature is ECM/neurite dysregulation as cell-intrinsic core, plus compositional neuropeptide shift, immunomodulation, and pH/nociception dysregulation. COL9A1 is the primary plasma-validation target."
keywords: "fibromyalgia, transcriptomics, biomarkers, COL9A1, E-value, sensitivity analysis"
